{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790319437,"nonce":"GNytkcO8r2iasZ/l8nWnnQ8rlpE=","title":"A stub keeps calling the baseline after its function is promoted","message":"A lazy function's stub calls `osr::published_entry(bead)` on every call. The entry is set when the first compile (or a quick baseline's replacement) publishes, and by a hot reload, but an optimizing-tier install sets only the call cell (`reload::set_call_target` in the promotion requester's force_promote closure and in the interpreter ladder), never the published entry. Whatever reaches the function through its stub rather than its cell keeps running the Cranelift baseline for the life of the program: data that holds the function's address (an in-place stub: a dispatch table, a vtable), a function value made before the first compile, a code pointer stored in a global.\n\nFound reading the code while fixing 42f4d19; not yet measured. crypto_pyaes's steady state runs no baseline code (entry counters stop growing once the LLVM tier is in), so it is not hit there. Worth a count of baseline entries in the steady state of kernels that dispatch through tables (richards, deltablue, chaos, go, the Lua method_call and op_overload benches) before changing it.\n\nFix candidate: publish the optimizing tier's entry as the bead's published entry too, where the function has one (the entry ABI is already the one both tiers share through the cell).","files":null}]}